<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian War of 1499–1504</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1499">1499</a>–<a href="/years/1504">1504</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Second Italian War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian War of 1499–1504 was fought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a>, who conquered the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1499 and then shared the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> with Ferdinand of Aragon. The partners fell out, and the Spanish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> defeated the French at Cerignola and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 1503. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> of 1504 left Naples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Milan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-war-of-1499-1504.jpg" alt="Italian War of 1499–1504" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toni Pecoraro · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battaglia_di_Cerignola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
